  "text": "**Solution 2 is wrong.** It treats isotropy weights as symmetric, so each weight α appears together with −α and is \"dependent.\" But isotropy weights are taken up to sign, one per invariant 2-plane, so ±α count as the same weight. The GKM condition then gives 2-independence, and the answer cannot be 1.\n\n**Solution 1 gives 5, which I believe is the intended answer, though its argument is loose.**\n- Maximal independence is at least 2 by the GKM condition, and it is exactly 2 for SU(3)/T, since α₁, α₂, α₁+α₂ are three weights in a 2-dimensional space.\n- It equals 3 for Gr(2,4) = SU(4)/S(U(2)×U(2)). The four isotropy weights e₁−e₃, e₁−e₄, e₂−e₃, e₂−e₄ are 3-independent, and the rectangle relation gives a 4-term dependence, so the value is exactly 3.\n- Solution 1's upper bound is shaky. CP^n = SU(n+1)/U(n) has n isotropy weights that are all linearly independent, so its independence is n for every n. This contradicts the blanket claim that maximal independence is always 2 or 3. The problem's sum is finite only if such spaces are excluded, and the solution's own step restricts to d > n. Under that restriction, S = {2, 3} is consistent with the examples above, which gives 2 + 3 = 5.\n\nI could not verify the classification against the source literature, so the confidence is moderate.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
